## 5. What the tool layer builds per call

    let content = ContentOperations::new(FontEnv::host(), SvgFonts::from_registry(&lane, &registry), Some(OcrContext::new(..)), cancel);
    let rows = mixdog_office_pdf::batch::apply_batch(&lane, &note, &path, &operations, &content)?;

* `ContentOperations` is made for one batch (it remembers which pages the batch has already wrapped in `q` ... `Q`, as
  pdf-lib does once per page object); `apply_batch` runs the content operations and `StructureOperations` through the
  one dispatcher of the adapter (`adapter::apply_pdf_batch`), so there is no second dispatcher. `ocr` and `cancel` are
  Node's batch `context` (`{ dataDir, signal }`); without an `OcrContext` an `ocr_pages` operation reports that it has
  none before it writes anything.
* The batch is blocking work (a lane job). `ocr_pages` runs the async recognition on a current-thread runtime of its
  own (as `security::secure_pdf` does with qpdf), so the lane the batch runs on must not be the one `OcrContext.lanes`
  submits its reads to when that lane has a single slot (`interactive_io`); run the batch on a write lane.
* `batch::PdfFormView` is the `adapter::FormView` the snapshot and the audit read a form through (the forms module's
  `describe_fields`, `minimum_field_size`, `rectangles_overlap`); the adapter's wire left it to "the forms module" and
  nothing had implemented it. It describes the fields on a copy of the document, because reading a form makes one.

## 6. Behaviour that differs from the Node code on purpose (nothing user-visible is lost)

* A mark by `find` measures the saved bytes and the batch continues on the same document value (Node reloaded the
  bytes because pdf-lib caches every stream it serializes; saving here does not change the document).
* `preview_fields` leaves the session document as it is (Node reloaded the same bytes).
* `flatten_form` takes each widget out of its page's annotations (Node removed the appearance reference, which is not
  in the list, and left the deleted widget's reference dangling).

## 7. Duplicated on purpose (files owned by other tasks)

`content_ops.rs` has its own `page_resources`, `sub_dictionary` and `content_refs` beside the ones in `ocr/layer.rs`
(private there, not this task's file): when the owner of `ocr/layer.rs` makes them `pub(crate)`, they can be removed
here. `flatten.rs` reads a field's parent itself for the same reason (`forms::parent_of` is private).

## 8. Tests

tests/twin_content_ops.rs (replays native/core/fixtures/office-content-ops/golden.json, 48 batches; `twin_live_content_ops`
is #[ignore]) and unit tests in marks.rs, content_ops.rs, flatten.rs and batch.rs. Not recorded: `ocr_pages` (its engine is
a download on first use).
